    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6710A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the public health law, in relation\nto the use of insect repellent by a child attending summer camp\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one amends the Public Health law by adding a new subdivision 5\nto section 1394. The proposed legislation requires each children's\novernight camp, summer day camp and travelling summer day camp to\nallow children attending such camp to carry and use insect for the\npurpose of decreasing the risk of Lyme disease and tick-borne\ninfections. For those insect repellents required to be registered with\nthe United State Environmental Protection Agency, prior written\npermission would be required. A record of such permission shall be\nmaintained in the camp office. This legislation would permit\nunlicensed personnel to assist a child unable to physically apply\ninsect repellent if permitted by a parent or guardian and authorized\nby the camp.\n\nSection two establishes that this law shall take effect immediately\nafter it shall have become law.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nSummer Camps are important partners in protecting children when they\nare away from home. Whether it is during the day or the entire summer,\nparents make an informed decision to entrust the care and supervision\nof their children to the camp they have selected.\n\nIn order for camps to fulfill their mission to provide a safe and fun\nsummer, camp must be able to protect their campers from Lyme disease\nand other tick-borne infections. Ensuring that insect repellent is\nreadily available and used responsibly by campers under the\nsupervision of counselors is paramount to a camper's safety.\n\nNew York State and the Department of Health has done a tremendous\namount of work to inform and advise the public of the dangers from\nLyme disease and other tick-borne infections. In fact, last year, the\nLegislature passed legislation, which the Governor signed into law,\ncreating a Lyme disease and tick-borne infection awareness and\nprevention program to, among other things, reduce exposure to Lyme\ndisease.\n\nThis legislation goes a long way to pursuing this end and is a common\nsense approach to protecting our young people, who are outside more\nfrequently and for much longer periods of time.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNone.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
